.TH THERMOTEST 1
.SH NAME
thermotest \- check thermo(2) against the identities and the efficiencies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B thermotest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Thermotest
checks
.IR thermo (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
Thermodynamics is unusually well supplied with things that must be
.I exactly
true rather than approximately so, and those are what is checked:
.IP
.EX
cp \- cv = R		a relation, not a definition
du = q \- w		on every process
wnet = qin \- qout	around every cycle
sum of q/T = 0		around a reversible cycle
.EE
.PP
The first law is a real check here and not a tautology, because
.IR thermo (2)
computes the work from the process and the heat from the process and
neither from the other.
.PP
The efficiencies are checked against their closed forms: Carnot at
0.6667 between 300 and 900 K, Otto at 0.5848 for a compression ratio of
9, Brayton at 0.4821 for a pressure ratio of 10.
The Otto figure is checked at three different heat inputs, four times
apart, and must not move \- that it depends on the compression ratio
alone is the surprising part of the result, and repeating the formula
would not test it.
.PP
The Diesel cycle is checked to be worse than the Otto at the same
compression ratio, and to
.I become
it as the cutoff ratio approaches one: 0.6316, then 0.6852 at a cutoff
of 1.001, against the Otto's 0.6853.
.SS Nothing beats Carnot
Every cycle is checked against the Carnot efficiency computed from its
.I own
hottest and coldest states, which is a bound the second law puts on it
and which no arithmetic mistake in the cycle is likely to respect.
The Carnot cycle itself meets that bound exactly and the others fall
short of it, which is the shape the answer has to have.
.SS Worked out twice
The heat exchanger is done both standard ways.
Effectiveness-NTU gives the outlet temperatures from the sizes; the log
mean temperature difference gives the duty from the temperatures.
They are different arithmetic and must give the same number, and they
agree to twelve figures for both arrangements.
Counterflow is separately checked to beat parallel flow, and parallel
flow to be stuck at 1/(1+\fIcr\fP) however large it is made.
.SS The critical point
The van der Waals constants are built from the critical temperature and
pressure, so asking for the critical point back is a round trip through
two formulas that are inverses.
And
.I z
at that point is exactly 3/8 for every van der Waals gas whatever its
constants, which is a coincidence a wrong inversion would break.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/thermotest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR thermo (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
Everything checked here is a property of the ideal-gas model, so a check
passing says the arithmetic is right and says nothing at all about
whether the model describes air.
.IR Thermo (2)'s
BUGS says where it does not.
.PP
The entropy sum around a cycle is checked for the Carnot cycle only,
where the heat crosses at two known temperatures.
For the other three the heat is added over a range of temperature and
the sum would need integrating, which is not done.
.PP
Nothing checks a process against its own reverse \- that expanding and
recompressing along the same path returns the original state exactly.
